+# Build loading and unloading
+
+## Performance report: server freeze when a big build loads or unloads
+
+**Workload**: a player with a 20,000-block saved build (the test build `SavedBuilds.luau`
+returns) joins or leaves a server with up to 8 players.
+
+**Bottleneck**: script time in `Builds.server.luau`. In the old code, `loadBuild` created every
+block in one frame. Each block was 3 instances (the `Part` plus an `Owner` `StringValue` and a
+`PlacedAt` `NumberValue`), so one join made 60,000 instances. Each part was parented before its
+properties were set (`Instance.new("Part", parent)`), so every property write went through
+replication, physics, and rendering. The server capture shows that frame: 2,100 ms under
+`Script_Builds`. Every client then got all 60,000 instances at once, which is the
+`Replicator ProcessPackets` hitch. On leave, `plotFolder:Destroy()` removed all of them in one
+frame, and every client processed that too.
+
+| Metric | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Longest server frame on join, 20,000 blocks | 2,100 ms (your capture) | not measured yet, see below |
+| Instances per loaded block | 3 | 1 |
+| Blocks built or destroyed per server frame | all of them | up to 200, within about 3 ms |
+
+I had no Studio session to measure in, so the "After" frame time still has to be captured. From
+the estimate: about 3 ms of build work per frame, plus a few ms in the join frame to reserve
+cells, instead of one 2-second frame.
+
+### Changes (highest level first)
+
+1. **Loading and unloading run over several frames.** One `Heartbeat` loop works through a
+   queue of jobs. Each frame it builds or destroys blocks until it hits 3 ms
+   (`JOB_TIME_ALLOWANCE`) or 200 blocks (`JOB_BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`). The time limit protects the
+   server frame. The block limit also caps how many instances each client has to process per
+   frame, which is what takes care of the client `ProcessPackets` hitch. All loading and
+   unloading players share one allowance, so 8 players joining together cost no more per frame
+   than one.
+2. **Owner and placed time live in server tables, not value objects.** `blockOwnerIds[part]`
+   and `blockPlacedAt[part]` replace the `Owner` and `PlacedAt` children. That makes a third of
+   the instances to create, replicate, store, and destroy (roughly 1,200 bytes less server
+   memory per block, about 190 MB less for 8 full builds).
+3. **Properties are set before `Parent`**, so each part replicates once, already set up.
+4. **The job loop has its own MicroProfiler label**, `Builds:RunJobs`, so you can find it in a
+   capture.
+
+### Behavior
+
+Same as before:
+
+- Placing: same checks (type, NaN, rate limit, reach, occupied cell, `Y >= 0`).
+- Removing: same checks. The owner check now reads `blockOwnerIds[block]` instead of the
+  `Owner` value. A block in a plot that is unloading has no owner any more, so nobody can remove
+  it, which is the same as before when the block was already gone.
+- Every block keeps its placed time: saved builds keep the saved `PlacedAt`, new blocks get
+  `os.time()`. The upcoming feature should read `blockPlacedAt[part]` in `Builds.server.luau`.
+  If clients need the time, send it on request or set it as an attribute on the part. Both cost
+  far less than a value object per block.
+
+**What players will notice:**
+
+- **A big build fills in over a few seconds instead of appearing at once.** It builds bottom
+  layer first, in save order. At the current limits a 20,000-block build takes at least
+  100 frames (about 1.7 s), and longer when the 3 ms limit runs out first. When several
+  players join together, the builds load one after another, so the last one can take several
+  times as long.
+- **A leaving player's build disappears over a similar time** instead of all at once.
+- **The server no longer freezes, and nobody rubber-bands**, when big builds load or unload.
+
+**Edge cases handled:**
+
+- All the cells a saved build needs are reserved in the join frame, so nobody (the owner
+  included) can place a block into a cell the build is still going to fill.
+- If the player leaves partway through loading, the load stops, the cells it hadn't built yet
+  are freed, and the blocks already built are destroyed.
+- When a player leaves, their cells and plot slot are freed at once, so a new player can get
+  that slot right away. For a moment, the new build can appear while the old one is still being
+  removed in the same space. Removing starts first and goes at the same rate as building, so
+  the overlap is short and visual only.
+- `loadBuild` checks the plot again after `SavedBuilds.load`, so it stays correct once that
+  call reads a DataStore and yields.
+
+### Left alone
+
+- `SavedBuilds.load` and cell reservation still run in the join frame. They only touch Luau
+  tables (no instances), probably a few ms for 20,000 blocks. Spread them over frames too if a
+  capture shows them.
+- Bigger design options for later: building block visuals on clients from compact data, and
+  `StreamingEnabled` so clients don't load builds they're far away from. Either one would cut
+  how much each client loads when it joins a full server (up to 160,000 parts). Neither is
+  needed to fix the freeze.
+
+### For you to verify
+
+- **Server**: capture the MicroProfiler while a 20,000-block player joins and leaves. The
+  longest frame should be close to normal, with `Builds:RunJobs` around 3 ms per frame.
+- **Clients**: check `Replicator ProcessPackets` while a big build loads, on a phone as well as
+  a desktop. If it still hitches, lower `JOB_BLOCKS_PER_FRAME`. If loading feels too slow and
+  frames have room left, raise both limits.
+- **In a playtest**: blocks fill in bottom-up, you can place and remove your own blocks during
+  and after loading, you can't remove anyone else's, and leaving mid-load leaves no blocks or
+  blocked cells behind.
